          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the preprints listed at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inter-universal+Teichm%C3%BCller+theory"} inter-universal Teichmüller theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shinichi+Mochizuki"} Shinichi Mochizuki
            has claimed, in particular, a proof of the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abc+conjecture"} abc conjecture
          %h2#reactions Reactions
          %h3#dimitrovs_commentary Dimitrov’s commentary
          %blockquote
            %p
              The following is verbatim quoted from
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Dimitrov12"} Dimitrov 2012
          %hr/
          %p
            %strong Completely rewritten. (9/26)
          %p It seems indeed that nothing like Theorem 1.10 from Mochizuki’s IUTT-IV could hold.
          %p
            Here is an infinite set of counterexamples, assuming for convenience two standard conjectures (the first being in fact a consequence of ABC), that contradict Thm. 1.10
            %em very
            badly.
          %p
            %em Assumptions:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A (Consequence of ABC)
                %em
                  For all but finitely many elliptic curves over
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℚ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                  and the minimal discriminant
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Δ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
                  satisfy
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi log
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi log
                          %mi N
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \log{|\Delta|} \lt (\log{N})^2
            %li
              %p
                B (Uniform Serre Open Image conjecture)
                %em For each
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in \mathbb{N}
                %em there is a constant
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c(d) \lt \infty
                %em such that for every number field
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi ℚ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F/\mathbb{Q}
                with
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi F
                        %mo :
                        %mi ℚ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [F:\mathbb{Q}] \leq d
                %em and every non-CM elliptic curve
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                %em over
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                %em and every prime
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℓ
                        %mo ≥
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell \geq c(d)
                %em the Galois representation of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_F
                %em on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi ℓ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E[\ell]
                %em has full image
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} GL
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi ℓ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/{\ell})
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                is held fixed. )
          %p
            Further, as far as I can tell from the proof of Theorem 1.10 of IUTTIV, the only reason for taking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tpd
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tpd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mn 5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F \coloneqq F_{\mathrm{tpd}}\big( \sqrt{-1}, E_{F_{\mathrm{tpd}}}[3\cdot 5]
                  \\big)
            — rather than simply
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tpd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \coloneqq F_{\mathrm{tpd}}(\sqrt{-1})
            — was to ensure that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            has semistable reduction over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %em Since I will only work in what follows with semistable elliptic curves over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            %em I will assume, for a mild technical convenience in the examples below, that for elliptic curves already semistable over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{\mathrm{tpd}}
            %em we may actually take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tpd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \coloneqq F_{\mathrm{tpd}}(\sqrt{-1})
            %em in Theorem 1.10.
          %p
            %em The infinite set of counterexamples.
            They come from Masser’s paper
            = succeed "Asterisque*" do
              %em Masser: Note on a conjecture of Szpiro,
            %em
              , as follows. Masser has produced an infinite set of Frey-Hellougarch (i.e., semistable and with rational 2-torsion) elliptic curves over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
              whose conductor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              and minimal discriminant
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
              satisfy
          #eq:eqone.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 6
                  %mi log
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≥
                  %mi log
                  %mi N
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %mi log
                        %mi N
                    %mrow
                      %mi log
                      %mrow
                        %mi log
                        %mi N
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{1}{6}\log{|\Delta|} \geq \log{N} + \frac{\sqrt{\log{N}}}{\log{\log{N}}}.
          %p
            (Thus,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            in these examples may be taken arbitrarily large. ) By (A) above, taking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            big enough will ensure that
          #eq:eqtwo.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi log
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi log
                  %mi N
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\log{|\Delta|} \lt (\log{N})^2.
          %p
            Next, the sum of the logarithms of the primes in the interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi log
                  %mi N
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi log
                  %mi N
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big( (\log{N})^2, 3(\log{N})^2 \big)
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi log
                    %mi N
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %mi o
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi log
                    %mi N
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2(\log{N})^2 + o((\log{N})^2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi log
                  %mi N
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gt (\log{N})^2
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo ≫
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \gg 0
            big enough. Thus, by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:eqtwo"} (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi log
                  %mi N
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi log
                  %mi N
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big( (\log{N})^2, 3(\log{N})^2 \big)
            contains a prime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℓ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
            which divides neither
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\Delta|
            nor any of the exponents
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ord
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha = \mathrm{ord}_p(\Delta)
            in the prime factorization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\Delta| = \prod p^{\alpha}
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\Delta|
          %p
            Consider now the pair
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,\ell)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} mod
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{\mathrm{mod}} = \mathbb{Q}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            has rational
            = succeed "-torsion," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tpd
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{\mathrm{tpd}} = \mathbb{Q}
            as well. Let
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F \coloneqq \mathbb{Q} \big(
                    \\sqrt{-1}\big)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            big enough, the pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E_F,\ell)
            arises from an
            %strong
              initial
              = succeed "-datum" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Θ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta
            as in IUTT-I, Definition 3.1. Indeed:
          %ul
            %li
              Certainly (a), (e), (f) of IUTT-I, Def. 3.1 are satisfied (with appropriate
              = succeed ");" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mi 𝕍
                        %mo ̲
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %munder
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo ̲
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{\mathbb{V}}, \, \underline{\epsilon}
            %li
              (b) of IUTT-I, Def. 3.1 is satisfied since by construction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              is semistable over
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℚ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            %li
              (c) of IUTT-I, Def. 3.1 is satisfied, in view of (B) above and the choice of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℓ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo ≫
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \gg 0
              is big enough (recall that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi log
                    %mi N
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell \gt (\log{N})^2
              by construction!), and by the observation that, for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              a place of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo =
                      %mi ℚ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msqrt
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{-1})
              = succeed "-adic" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              = succeed "-parameter" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              equals
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ord
                        %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{ord}_v (\Delta)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ord
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{ord}_p(\Delta)
              for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                      %mi p
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \mid p \gt 2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ord
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2\cdot\mathrm{ord}_2(\Delta)
              for
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \mid 2
          %p
            while
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝕍
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} mod
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} bad
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{V}_{\mathrm{mod}}^{\mathrm{bad}}
            consists of the primes dividing
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
          %ul
            %li
              Finally, (d) of IUTT-I, Def. 3.1 is satisfied upon excluding at most four of Masser’s examples
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          %p
            %strong> Now
            , take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi log
                  %mi N
                  %msup
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \coloneqq \big( \log{N} \big)^{-2}
            in Theorem 1.10 of IUTT-IV; this is certainly permissible for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo ≫
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \gg 0
            large enough.
            %em
              I claim that the conclusion of Theorem 1.10 contradicts
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:eqone"} (1)
              as soon as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo ≫
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \gg 0
              is large enough.
          %p
            For note that Mochizuki’s quantity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi log
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔮
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \log(\mathfrak{q})
            is precisely
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi log
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \log{|\Delta|}
            (reference: see e.g. Szpiro’s article in the Grothendieck Festschrift, vol. 3); his
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi log
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔡
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \log{(\mathfrak{d}^{\mathrm{tpd}})}
            is zero; his
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_{\mathrm{mod}}
            is
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi log
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔣
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tpd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \log{(\mathfrak{f}^{\mathrm{tpd}})}
            is our
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi log
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \log{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi log
                  %mi N
                  %msup
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \coloneqq \big( \log{N} \big)^{-2}
            then makes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/\ell \lt \epsilon
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell \lt 3/\epsilon
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 6
                  %mi log
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mn 29
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi log
                  %mi N
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                  %mi log
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %msup
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 8
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi log
                  %mi N
                  %mo +
                  %mn 16
                  %mi log
                  %mrow
                    %mi log
                    %mi N
                  %mo +
                  %mn 32
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{1}{6} \log{|\Delta|} \leq (1+29\epsilon) \cdot \log{N} + 2\log{(3\epsilon^{-8})}
                  \\lt \log{N} + 16\log{\log{N}} + 32,
          %p
            where we have used
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mi log
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi log
                  %mi N
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \log{N} = (\log{N})^{-1} \lt 1
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \gt 3
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi log
                    %mn 3
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2\log{3} \lt 3
          %p
            %em
              The last display contradicts
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:eqone"} (1)
              as soon as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo ≫
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \gg 0
              is big enough.
          %p Thus Masser’s examples yield infinitely many counterexamples to Theorem 1.10 of IUTT-IV (as presently written).
          %p
            %strong Added on 10/15.
            Mochizuki has commented on the apparent contradiction between Masser’s examples and Theorem 1.10:
          %p &lt;http://www.kurims.kyoto-u.ac.jp/~motizuki/Inter-universal%20Teichmuller%20Theory%20IV%20(comments).pdf&gt;
          %p
            (Point (4.) in those Comments was subsequently modified twice. ) He writes that he will revise portions of IUTT-III and IUTT-IV, and will make them available in the near future. Taking into account the latest version of point (4.) from Mochizuki’s Comments, here is the anticipated revised version of Theorem 1.10 (after taking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo ∼
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℓ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \sim 1/\ell
            \- which is essentially optimal - and not worrying about the best constants or the most general version):
          %p
            Take the pair
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E,\ell)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E/\mathbb{Q}
            is a semistable elliptic curve with (say, for the sake of simplifying) rational
            = succeed "-torsion" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            and conductor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            (square-free). Assume that:
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℓ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
              divides neither
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              nor the any of the exponents in the prime factorization of
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            %li
              The Galois representation of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℚ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msqrt
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{-1})}
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E[\ell]
              has full image
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} GL
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi ℓ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/\ell)
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  Conjecturally, this condition should only exclude a finite list of values of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℓ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi E
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %li
              The
              = succeed "-adic" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
              is bounded. (
              %em> Assume this for simplicity in the statement below. It is not truly an essential condition either
              ).
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mo =
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi log
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell = O(\log{N})
              (Such a choice, compliant with the three previous conditions, can be ensured; see Section 2 in IUTT-IV, or the paper [GenEll]).
          %p
            Let
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                        %mi N
                        %mo ,
                        %mi p
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi ℓ
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \coloneqq \prod_{p \mid N,  p \lt \ell} p
            = succeed "," do
              %em Then
            = succeed "):" do
              %em imply
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 6
                  %mi log
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 200
                    %mi ℓ
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} )
                  %mi log
                  %mi N
                  %mo +
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi log
                  %mrow
                    %mi log
                    %mi N
                  %mo −
                  %mi log
                  %mi M
                  %mo +
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi log
                  %mrow
                    %mi log
                    %mi N
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{1}{6} \log{\Delta} \lt \Big( 1 + \frac{200}{\ell} \Big) \log{N}  + \omega(M) \cdot \log{\log{N}} - \log{M} + O\big( \log{\log{N}} \big),
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega(\cdot)
            denotes “number of prime factors.” If we take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi log
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell \sim \log{N}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega(M)
            bounded (i.e., restrict to conductors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            which are only divisible by a bounded number of primes
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi log
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt \log{N}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 6
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi log
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi log
                    %mi N
                    %mo +
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi log
                    %mrow
                      %mi log
                      %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1/6) \log{\Delta} \lt \log{N} + O(\log{\log{N}})
            = succeed "?" do
              %em
                Must this be true for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                a large enough square-free integer such that the number of primes
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi log
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt \log{N}
                dividing
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                is bounded
            = succeed "-triple," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi abc
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} abc
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            is
            %em essentially
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi abc
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (abc)^2
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo =
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} rad
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi abc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N = \mathrm{rad}(abc)
          %p
            A side remark: note that the inverse
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℓ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/\ell
            of the prime level from the de Rham-Etale correspondence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo †
                  %mo ,
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↔
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ℓ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E^{\dagger}, \lt \ell) \leftrightarrow E[\ell]
            in Mochizuki’s “Hodge-Arakelov theory” ultimately figures as the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            in the ABC conjecture.
          %p
            %em (I have deleted the remainder of the 10/15 Addendum, since it is now obsolete after Mochizuki’s revised comments.)
          %p
            %strong Added on 3-13-13.
            Mochizuki has posted revisions of his second and third papers on Inter-Universal Teichmuller Theory. They can be found at the bottom of his webpage
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.kurims.kyoto-u.ac.jp/~motizuki/papers-english.html"} here
          %hr/
          %blockquote
            %p
              end of verbatim quote from
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Dimitrov12"} Dimitrov 2012
          %h3#scholze__stixs_comment Scholze &amp; Stix’s comment
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ScholzeStix18"} Scholze &amp; Stix 2018
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abc+conjecture"} abc conjecture
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mochizuki%27s+corollary+3.12"} Mochizuki's corollary 3.12
          %h2#references References
          %p
            For
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shinichi+Mochizuki"} Shinichi Mochizuki
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inter-universal+Teichm%C3%BCller+theory"} inter-universal Teichmüller theory
          %p Commentary on this proof by other authors:
          %ul
            %li#Dimitrov12
              %p
                Vesselin Dimitrov, MO comment 2012 (
                %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/a/107279/381"}> MO:a/107279
                )
            %li#ScholzeStix18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Scholze"}> Peter Scholze
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jakob+Stix"} Jakob Stix
